%N A400814 Number of minor star triples in the level-n supertile of the Spectre tiling.
%C A400814 Introduced in 2023 by Smith, Myers, Kaplan and Goodman-Strauss, the Spectre monotile is a chiral aperiodic monotile: a single tile that tiles the plane only aperiodically, without using its mirror image. The level-n supertile is obtained from a single Spectre tile by n inflation steps of the S8/S9 substitution (see Smith et al., Figure 2.2) and contains A057080(n) tiles.
%C A400814 A star triple is a set of three Spectre tiles related by a rotation through 120 degrees about a common point of their boundaries. Up to orientation, there are exactly two such configurations, the major star triple (A400813), with the larger circumradius, and the minor star triple, with the smaller one. Star triples first appear at inflation level 3.
%C A400814 Minor star triples are the minority of all star triples; their ratio to the major star triples in the infinite tiling is (sqrt(15)-1)/(sqrt(15)+1). The minor star triple is the central star triple of the Type C megastars (A400810) and the first star triple of the clusters of family A (A397115). Tables, growth rules and figures are given in Horvat, "Star triples in the Spectre tiling: tables" (see Links).
%C A400814 The recurrence and formulas were derived from the growth rules of the cluster families and megaobjects, which were found empirically from direct counts in the supertiles of levels 3 to 7 (see A400812); they are not proved.
%C A400814 a(0)-a(9) were confirmed by direct counts in the level-n supertiles built exactly from the S8/S9 substitution, and a(10)-a(11) by adding to the counts in the child supertiles the star triples that cross their seams. - _Alper Ferudun_, Oct 22 2026
